WEBVTT

00:00.040 --> 00:03.000
This is what my wife calls my Bible salesman photo.

00:04.900 --> 00:05.380
Yeah.

00:06.420 --> 00:07.240
Accurate, yes?

00:08.180 --> 00:08.620
Yeah.

00:09.160 --> 00:10.220
No lies detected.

00:11.300 --> 00:14.180
So I've got a little presentation here I want to walk you through

00:14.180 --> 00:16.480
that I think I'm able to click my way to.

00:16.680 --> 00:17.280
No, wait.

00:17.480 --> 00:18.840
I'm clicking the wrong slides.

00:19.620 --> 00:20.600
My friend's in the back.

00:20.900 --> 00:21.440
Can we flip?

00:23.060 --> 00:25.240
Anyone else take the Acela up this morning?

00:26.060 --> 00:28.320
I was on the 5 o'clock from Penn Station.

00:28.320 --> 00:30.320
So if I get a little delirious, that's why.

00:31.280 --> 00:31.700
All right.

00:31.740 --> 00:32.960
We're waiting for the deck.

00:34.240 --> 00:35.940
We're going to take questions at the end.

00:36.260 --> 00:38.260
And you can ask me about my presentation, really,

00:38.340 --> 00:40.020
anything you want to know about Reuters News.

00:40.360 --> 00:44.780
I work in Times Square every single day, five days a week.

00:45.380 --> 00:48.640
And there you find the New York newsroom of Reuters News,

00:48.820 --> 00:50.740
which I think you probably have some familiarity with,

00:50.760 --> 00:53.540
one of the world's largest news organizations with people all around it.

00:54.140 --> 00:56.720
And I'm here to introduce you to a new project that we are,

00:56.720 --> 01:01.440
I think, publicly speaking about for the first time today at this event.

01:01.600 --> 01:03.300
And we'll launch in the coming weeks.

01:03.440 --> 01:04.680
This is kind of a sneak peek.

01:04.820 --> 01:06.820
And we call it the Reuters Climate Monitor.

01:07.480 --> 01:08.300
So what is that?

01:08.760 --> 01:10.420
I'm going to talk you through who I am,

01:10.800 --> 01:12.120
kind of what my team does,

01:12.560 --> 01:16.060
and then this new approach we're taking towards climate data as we go.

01:17.400 --> 01:19.020
Please put your questions in.

01:19.560 --> 01:19.760
All right.

01:19.780 --> 01:20.580
So I'm Ben Welsh.

01:20.880 --> 01:21.900
I'm from Iowa.

01:22.240 --> 01:23.380
Any other Hawkeyes here?

01:23.380 --> 01:27.600
All right.

01:27.680 --> 01:29.560
Any other Caitlin Clark fans here?

01:29.740 --> 01:30.520
Can I go for that?

01:30.760 --> 01:31.420
Yeah, okay.

01:33.160 --> 01:36.380
And I've been working at Reuters in New York for three years now,

01:36.500 --> 01:38.920
and I'm one of these nerds in the newsroom.

01:39.020 --> 01:42.100
I'm a reporter, an editor, and a computer programmer.

01:42.440 --> 01:46.520
And I lead a specialized team that works on automating data

01:46.520 --> 01:49.420
to cover breaking news for our service.

01:49.760 --> 01:50.660
What does that mean?

01:50.660 --> 01:51.720
Well, I'll give you an example.

01:51.820 --> 01:55.680
So our goal is really to build off these types of data applications

01:55.680 --> 01:57.140
that have become part of news,

01:57.240 --> 01:59.060
but you might not always think of as software.

01:59.480 --> 02:02.240
These are the live election results we look at on election night, right?

02:02.620 --> 02:04.680
I bet people in this room spent a lot of time

02:04.680 --> 02:07.720
refreshing their local coronavirus tracker, you know,

02:07.980 --> 02:09.340
when we were in the middle of the pandemic.

02:09.340 --> 02:13.640
And those data apps have become kind of almost by accident

02:13.640 --> 02:17.660
some of the most successful things that news organizations publish

02:17.660 --> 02:19.920
on their dot-com websites and in their apps.

02:20.660 --> 02:24.020
And what teams like mine at all the different news organizations

02:24.020 --> 02:27.180
really around the world have tried to do in the last few years

02:27.180 --> 02:31.240
is kind of retool and create miniature assembly lines

02:31.240 --> 02:33.000
inside of our information factories

02:33.000 --> 02:36.280
that kind of manufacture these types of products, right?

02:36.280 --> 02:38.920
And they're really just automated software systems,

02:39.040 --> 02:42.060
data pipelines that go to your local election office, right?

02:42.160 --> 02:45.540
Gather the results, bring them back, clean them up,

02:45.920 --> 02:48.720
and then put them up on a beautiful map

02:48.720 --> 02:50.500
before your eyes, right?

02:50.520 --> 02:51.240
You're familiar with this.

02:51.300 --> 02:53.340
And that approach works not just for elections,

02:53.340 --> 02:55.740
but really for all kinds of different stuff.

02:56.260 --> 02:59.400
And so my group is this new little assembly line inside Reuters

02:59.400 --> 03:02.520
trying to manufacture as many of those widgets as we can.

03:02.520 --> 03:05.920
And so like an example that is on our homepage

03:05.920 --> 03:07.620
like five or six times a week

03:07.620 --> 03:09.300
is we have all these automations

03:09.300 --> 03:12.080
that go get the latest economics and financial data

03:12.080 --> 03:14.680
from like a kind of business terminal type situation

03:14.680 --> 03:16.020
or a government website.

03:16.420 --> 03:18.320
And it doesn't just bring them down

03:18.320 --> 03:21.140
and allow us to analyze them.

03:21.180 --> 03:23.600
It goes all the way through out into live charts

03:23.600 --> 03:25.340
that go up onto our website, right?

03:25.360 --> 03:26.620
So we've got hundreds and hundreds

03:26.620 --> 03:28.240
of these breaking news automations

03:28.240 --> 03:31.580
that help us cover markets, economics, public opinion,

03:31.580 --> 03:32.360
other things.

03:32.460 --> 03:33.880
And that's been a success for us.

03:34.420 --> 03:35.340
And so we're trying to,

03:35.900 --> 03:38.360
and so we try to aim for the biggest stories

03:38.360 --> 03:40.080
so that when we invest in these automations,

03:40.180 --> 03:42.080
we can get continual use of them

03:42.080 --> 03:43.880
and really cover the top stuff.

03:44.000 --> 03:46.740
So the last few weeks, a lot of oil prices charts, right?

03:46.780 --> 03:47.740
I've been sitting on our homepage

03:47.740 --> 03:49.180
coming out of these automations.

03:49.680 --> 03:52.400
And we're looking to expand that approach

03:52.400 --> 03:54.640
beyond just stuff having to do with money

03:54.640 --> 03:56.120
and markets and that kind of thing

03:56.120 --> 03:58.240
into other major breaking news events,

03:58.340 --> 04:01.400
which of course extreme weather is a great example of, right?

04:01.400 --> 04:06.040
And so last year we rolled out a new hurricane tracking system

04:06.040 --> 04:08.240
that allows us to watch cyclones

04:08.240 --> 04:10.320
not just around Florida in the United States

04:10.320 --> 04:12.700
but really in Asia and elsewhere in the world

04:12.700 --> 04:14.820
so that we're able to cover those events

04:14.820 --> 04:17.880
kind of as they happen in this same style.

04:19.460 --> 04:23.060
And our next goal and what we've been working on

04:23.060 --> 04:25.060
is bringing that approach to climate

04:25.060 --> 04:28.800
and to the stuff this conference is about.

04:28.800 --> 04:30.680
So this guy right here, this is my boss.

04:30.780 --> 04:31.760
This is Simon Robinson.

04:31.920 --> 04:33.080
Don't tell him I'm doing this.

04:33.620 --> 04:37.280
And he's the executive editor of Reuters in London.

04:37.440 --> 04:39.500
And he came to me with a challenge

04:39.500 --> 04:43.100
which was could our newsroom use data

04:43.100 --> 04:45.240
and this approach I've been telling you about

04:45.240 --> 04:48.500
to cover the day-to-day kind of impact of climate change

04:48.500 --> 04:52.360
because he really saw a kind of gap in our coverage.

04:52.360 --> 04:55.500
If you think about you have a major heat wave

04:55.500 --> 04:57.020
in the Mediterranean last summer

04:57.020 --> 04:59.540
and for us at Reuters that's a big story

04:59.540 --> 05:02.120
that's going to dominate the top of the file,

05:02.340 --> 05:05.300
the homepage every day for maybe even a week, right?

05:05.580 --> 05:07.540
But in that coverage we only have

05:07.540 --> 05:09.700
the kind of traditional weather and temperature data

05:09.700 --> 05:13.360
and it just seemed to him that there's this kind of gap

05:13.360 --> 05:16.120
where the context of climate

05:16.120 --> 05:18.740
is kind of missing from those major storylines.

05:18.740 --> 05:23.140
And so his challenge was how could we try to address that

05:23.140 --> 05:24.640
or could we try to address that?

05:25.200 --> 05:27.280
And it was the first time I really thought about it

05:27.280 --> 05:29.860
and, you know, I definitely want feedback from you all

05:29.860 --> 05:31.160
on how we're doing as we go through this

05:31.160 --> 05:32.440
because you know more about it than me.

05:33.040 --> 05:35.280
But it seemed to me that one of the problems was

05:35.280 --> 05:37.880
is that unlike the stock market data

05:37.880 --> 05:39.200
or the oil prices data,

05:39.680 --> 05:41.860
which updates every second, right,

05:41.900 --> 05:43.600
almost around the clock, right,

05:43.720 --> 05:46.800
a lot of climate data is a little slower moving, right,

05:47.100 --> 05:48.220
where it might tell you,

05:48.220 --> 05:51.940
well, the Earth's temperature was this last month

05:51.940 --> 05:55.180
or last year, not today or tomorrow, right?

05:55.740 --> 05:57.300
And a lot of climate data,

05:57.580 --> 05:59.300
for very good scientific reasons,

05:59.660 --> 06:02.400
is measured in these kind of arcane statistics

06:02.400 --> 06:05.740
that aren't how the average person thinks about the weather.

06:06.000 --> 06:08.240
The average temperature is a great example, right,

06:08.280 --> 06:10.480
where the most climate goals, as I understand it,

06:10.500 --> 06:11.860
are set using the average temperature,

06:12.020 --> 06:13.020
but if I asked any of you,

06:13.160 --> 06:15.260
what's the average temperature in Boston today,

06:15.680 --> 06:17.180
you probably never even thought about it, right?

06:17.180 --> 06:18.920
People think in highs and lows.

06:19.680 --> 06:23.240
And so my feeling was that if we're going to address this,

06:23.300 --> 06:24.580
we had to take those things on.

06:25.200 --> 06:26.840
And so I went out to kind of,

06:26.960 --> 06:28.620
on a little reporting trip to see,

06:29.180 --> 06:31.240
could we get faster climate data

06:31.240 --> 06:33.540
that is in more relatable statistics

06:33.540 --> 06:36.160
and that isn't just entirely global either,

06:36.260 --> 06:38.440
but maybe can relate to people like where they live,

06:38.520 --> 06:39.720
like the weather report does.

06:39.720 --> 06:42.620
And so we surveyed what everybody else did,

06:42.720 --> 06:44.500
I interviewed a bunch of nerdy nerds,

06:44.500 --> 06:46.840
and we found the answer is yes,

06:46.880 --> 06:49.420
it actually might be possible to pull off such a thing.

06:49.920 --> 06:51.840
And if you guys want to get into all the details

06:51.840 --> 06:54.240
of the era five data set and all that stuff,

06:54.280 --> 06:55.300
I'm happy to go down the road,

06:55.300 --> 06:58.220
but we kind of came up with a rough methodology

06:58.220 --> 07:01.880
that we think will allow us just getting started

07:01.880 --> 07:03.680
in our first steps into this coverage

07:03.680 --> 07:05.680
to create a daily dashboard

07:05.680 --> 07:09.360
that automatically compares today's temperature,

07:09.540 --> 07:10.520
tomorrow's temperature,

07:10.900 --> 07:12.200
against the historic record,

07:12.560 --> 07:13.880
against what used to be normal

07:13.880 --> 07:16.100
before widespread global climate change

07:16.100 --> 07:17.880
for Boston, for Swisher, Iowa,

07:18.060 --> 07:18.720
where I grew up,

07:18.980 --> 07:20.420
for anywhere on earth, right?

07:20.420 --> 07:23.060
And then deliver a comprehensive view

07:23.060 --> 07:27.080
of today's, how weird today's temperature is, right?

07:27.100 --> 07:28.540
The thing we all ask ourselves

07:28.540 --> 07:30.900
when we're close to one of these events

07:30.900 --> 07:33.220
by measuring what people call an anomaly,

07:33.540 --> 07:34.960
just the difference between today

07:34.960 --> 07:36.080
and what used to be normal.

07:37.200 --> 07:40.280
And we've created and we're preparing to launch

07:40.280 --> 07:42.280
a color-coded world map

07:42.280 --> 07:45.520
that will update each day and highlight right now

07:45.520 --> 07:48.740
where the most significant temperature anomalies are on earth,

07:48.740 --> 07:51.340
the interactive controls will allow you

07:51.340 --> 07:54.000
to search your hometown, where your mom lives,

07:54.500 --> 07:55.300
wherever you'd like to go,

07:55.360 --> 07:56.440
whatever you're interested in,

07:56.600 --> 07:58.060
and find out the differences there

07:58.060 --> 07:59.880
in the same way that the weather report can.

08:00.440 --> 08:02.580
We're calling it the Reuters Climate Monitor,

08:03.540 --> 08:05.060
and we have a prototype

08:05.060 --> 08:07.720
that this is the screenshot of

08:07.720 --> 08:09.240
that we're preparing to launch.

08:10.000 --> 08:11.660
And we were going to try maybe

08:11.660 --> 08:13.380
to open up the webpage in the back,

08:13.740 --> 08:15.920
and this is just a quick look at the demo page,

08:16.020 --> 08:17.420
you know, where you're able to see

08:17.420 --> 08:19.580
today's kind of forecast,

08:19.920 --> 08:21.080
the red areas are higher,

08:21.180 --> 08:21.880
the blue are lower,

08:22.380 --> 08:24.800
and you'd be able to search anywhere you wanted

08:24.800 --> 08:26.640
and zoom in and get a report, right?

08:27.520 --> 08:28.680
We can jump back.

08:32.060 --> 08:34.120
And that's just the beginning, right?

08:34.500 --> 08:36.940
So after we get this up and out,

08:37.140 --> 08:38.900
we have immediate plans to follow on

08:38.900 --> 08:40.840
with stuff that we hope will make it more newsy.

08:41.100 --> 08:43.340
We're going to try to calculate the number of people

08:43.340 --> 08:45.740
living in extreme conditions every day,

08:45.900 --> 08:47.700
today, tomorrow, the day after,

08:48.060 --> 08:49.540
so that we're able to roll up

08:49.540 --> 08:52.720
those summaries of how many million people, etc.,

08:52.720 --> 08:53.980
into our coverage.

08:54.320 --> 08:57.060
And that, to me, is delivering that type of context

08:57.060 --> 08:59.020
that Simon thought was missing from those reports.

08:59.400 --> 09:02.120
So on the very day that we're writing about,

09:02.640 --> 09:04.200
an unusual temperature event somewhere,

09:04.300 --> 09:07.120
we're able to get that context automatically in, right?

09:07.940 --> 09:09.620
We're going to try to forecast it out

09:09.620 --> 09:10.520
further into the future,

09:11.060 --> 09:12.480
and we're going to use this analysis,

09:12.760 --> 09:14.560
we actually currently are using this analysis

09:14.560 --> 09:18.300
to kind of fling correspondence around the world

09:18.300 --> 09:21.440
to areas where we've seen significant climate change

09:21.440 --> 09:23.160
already bubbling up out of this data

09:23.160 --> 09:24.600
over the last 30, 40 years,

09:24.800 --> 09:27.540
and report on the impacts of that.

09:27.720 --> 09:29.200
And so it's going to serve as a reporting tool

09:29.200 --> 09:31.480
for enterprise as well.

09:33.740 --> 09:34.740
I want to hear from you.

09:34.740 --> 09:37.820
So this is just the very beginning.

09:38.000 --> 09:39.920
It might seem kind of basic to you guys.

09:39.980 --> 09:40.240
I don't know.

09:40.280 --> 09:41.040
I'm curious to learn.

09:41.580 --> 09:42.780
But in this room with experts,

09:42.940 --> 09:45.700
I would love to know answers to questions like these

09:45.700 --> 09:48.940
and how you think a news organization like Reuters

09:48.940 --> 09:53.140
could and should or ought to better use data

09:53.140 --> 09:55.280
to cover climate change.

09:55.940 --> 09:56.600
Thank you.

09:56.600 --> 09:58.660
Thank you.

09:58.660 --> 09:59.140
Thank you.

09:59.340 --> 09:59.880
Thank you.

10:00.060 --> 10:01.760
Thank you.

10:01.760 --> 10:31.740
Thank you.
